        <div id="how-we-chose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. How We Chose These Treadmills</h2>
<p>
            We have not tested these treadmills, and nothing on this page should be read as if we had.
            Our reviews of the
            <a href="/bowflex-t6-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Bowflex T6</a> and the
            Horizon, Bowflex T9 and Life Fitness machines are specification reviews, not hands-on tests.
            The picks were made from manufacturer pages, owner's manuals and specification sheets,
            retailer listings, independent reviews and Apple's own support guide, all checked on
            15 October 2026.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The eligibility rule.</strong> Apple's guide to using gym equipment with Apple Watch
            explains that compatible machines carry a "Connects to Apple Watch" or "Connect to Apple
            Watch" label, and it describes the pairing steps, but it does not name brands or models. So
            we could not check a treadmill against an Apple list. Instead, a machine qualified only
            where its manufacturer states Apple Watch tap-to-connect for that model and an independent
            source confirms it. For the Bowflex T6 and T9 the manufacturer source is the owner's manual,
            which walks through the pairing step by step; for the Horizon 7.0 AT it is Horizon's
            product page, which lists Apple GymKit for this model and not for its siblings; for the Run
            CX it is Life Fitness's specification sheet and store listing for the Track Connect 2.0
            console.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>What fell out.</strong> Horizon's own comparison table lists the 7.4 AT and 7.8 AT
            as not connecting to Apple Watch or Samsung Galaxy Watch, so they are not here despite being
            good treadmills. Life Fitness consoles older than Track Connect 2.0 are excluded; Life
            Fitness's sheet says the enhanced Bluetooth features are not compatible with previous
            models, and a retailer notes the original Track Connect console lacks phone pairing.
            Commercial gym treadmills from brands that have supported GymKit in clubs were left out on
            price and because we could not confirm a current home listing with GymKit stated. Any
            listing that said only "works with Apple Health" or "syncs to Apple Watch" through an app
            was treated as not GymKit.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Safety check.</strong> We searched the US Consumer Product Safety Commission's
            recall database by brand and by product type. The recalls we found for these makers were
            for other models — an older Horizon T101 version in 2022, and Matrix treadmills from the
            same parent company — and none covered a machine on this page. Specifications and prices
            are as listed on 15 October 2026; treadmill consoles change between production runs, so
            confirm the console version and Apple Watch support for the exact unit you are buying.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="three-ways" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. GymKit, Bluetooth FTMS or a Plain Indoor Run: Three Different Things</h2>
<p>
            Listings blur these together. They are three separate ways for a watch, a phone and a
            treadmill to relate, and only the first is what people usually mean by "treadmill
            compatible with Apple Watch".
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">1. Apple GymKit: the watch and the treadmill share one workout</h3>
          <p>
            GymKit is Apple's system for pairing an Apple Watch directly with compatible cardio
            equipment. Apple's guide says to turn on Detect Gym Equipment in the watch's Workout
            settings, hold the watch within a few centimetres of the machine's contactless reader with
            the display facing it, wait for a gentle tap and a beep, then start the workout on the
            treadmill. Stopping the treadmill ends the workout. Bowflex's manuals describe the same
            sequence: hold the watch on the icon on the console for about five seconds, choose a
            workout type on the watch, wait for it to read "Start Treadmill", then press Start.
          </p>
          <p>
            The point of all this is where the numbers come from. With GymKit, distance and speed come
            from the treadmill rather than being estimated from your wrist, and your heart rate goes the
            other way, onto the console. Horizon says its connection syncs calories, speed, distance,
            pace and time; the 2024 launch announcement for the 7.0 AT and Bowflex T9 listed heart
            rate, active and total calories, speed, distance, pace and incline. The finished workout
            lands in the Fitness app like any other.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">2. Bluetooth FTMS: the treadmill talks to an app, not to the watch</h3>
          <p>
            FTMS — the Bluetooth Fitness Machine Service — is an open standard that lets a treadmill
            broadcast its speed, incline and distance to an app on a phone or tablet: Zwift, Kinomap,
            the Peloton app and similar. All four GymKit machines here also have FTMS, and so do many
            treadmills without GymKit. FTMS is about apps on your screen. It does not, on its own, make
            a treadmill pair with an Apple Watch, and a listing that says "Bluetooth" or "FTMS" is not
            telling you anything about GymKit. Some apps write their workouts to Apple Health, which can
            make a non-GymKit treadmill's data turn up next to your watch data — useful, but a different
            route with its own quirks. Our
            <a href="/zwift-treadmill-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Zwift treadmill guide</a>
            covers the FTMS side in detail.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">3. An Indoor Run or Indoor Walk with no pairing at all</h3>
          <p>
            This works on every treadmill. Start an Indoor Run or Indoor Walk in the Workout app and
            step on. Heart rate comes from the watch's sensor exactly as it would outdoors; distance is
            an estimate from the watch's motion sensors, because there is no GPS indoors. The estimate
            is the weak point — holding the handrails, carrying something or an unusual arm swing all
            affect it — and our guide to
            <a href="/how-to-connect-apple-watch-to-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">connecting an Apple Watch to a treadmill</a>
            explains how to correct the distance at the end of the session so your history stays
            honest.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The practical difference</strong> is mainly distance and pace accuracy, and a bit of
            convenience. GymKit takes the treadmill's figures; an Indoor Run guesses. But "the
            treadmill's figures" are only as good as the treadmill's speed calibration, which is the
            subject of the next section but one.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="is-gymkit-worth-it" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Is GymKit Worth Choosing a Treadmill For?</h2>
<p>
            Sometimes. The answer depends on how you use your watch data and what else you want from
            the machine.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>It is worth weighting heavily if</strong> you close your Activity rings religiously,
            track your running in the Fitness app, and want treadmill distance and pace recorded
            without fiddling. It also suits households where several people use the treadmill and each
            has their own watch: tap on, and each person's workout goes to their own watch. And if you
            use Apple Fitness+, Bowflex's manuals include a set of steps for running a Fitness+
            treadmill workout with the watch paired; Fitness+ is a separate subscription, so check the
            current price.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>It is a pleasant extra rather than a reason to buy if</strong> you mainly look at
            heart rate, or you already log runs through another app. The watch records heart rate
            identically with or without GymKit. If your distance only needs to be roughly right, an
            Indoor Run and a quick correction at the end does the job on any treadmill.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>It should not override the basics.</strong> A machine that pairs beautifully but has
            a deck too short for your stride, a weight rating too close to your weight, or a console you
            dislike is the wrong machine. The four GymKit treadmills here all happen to be sound running
            treadmills with 60-inch decks and 15% incline, which is why the list is short but still
            useful. If you find a cheaper or smaller machine advertising Apple Watch support, check
            whether it means GymKit or simply an app, and check the basics first.
          </p>
          <p>
            One more realistic expectation: reviewers and owners report that GymKit pairing is usually
            quick but not flawless. One independent reviewer of the 7.0 AT found pairing easy but
            reported the watch occasionally disconnecting mid-workout and reconnecting without trouble.
            Treat the link as convenient, not infallible, and check the workout saved before you step
            off.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="accuracy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Accuracy: GymKit Records the Treadmill’s Numbers, Right or Wrong</h2>
<p>
            The quiet catch with GymKit is that it hands your watch whatever the treadmill believes.
            If the console reads 6.0 mph and the belt is really doing 5.7, your Fitness app will record
            the faster pace with complete confidence, every session, for as long as you own the
            machine. An Indoor Run has the opposite problem — it is an estimate from your wrist — but at
            least it does not inherit a systematic treadmill error.
          </p>
          <p>
            New machines are usually close, and the error tends to grow as belts stretch and decks wear.
            So in the first week, while the returns window is open, check the belt speed with a marked
            belt and a stopwatch. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-calibration/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill calibration guide</a>
            walks through it in about ten minutes, and
            <a href="/is-my-treadmill-speed-accurate/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">whether your treadmill speed is accurate</a>
            explains why a treadmill, a watch and a foot pod rarely agree out of the box.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you run on a treadmill without GymKit and want better pace than the wrist estimate,
            a foot pod paired to the watch is the usual answer; our
            <a href="/treadmill-foot-pod-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill foot pod guide</a>
            covers how they work and how to calibrate one against a checked belt.
          </p>
          <p>
            Heart rate deserves its own note. GymKit sends the watch's heart rate to the console, so
            the reading on the screen is the watch's wrist reading, with the strengths and limits of
            any optical sensor at hard efforts. If you train by heart rate zones and find the wrist
            unreliable at speed, a chest strap is the standard fix; our comparison of
            <a href="/chest-strap-vs-wrist-heart-rate/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">chest strap and wrist heart rate</a>
            covers the trade-off.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-to-check-a-listing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. How to Check a Listing for Real Apple Watch Support</h2>
<p>
            Treadmill listings use "Apple Watch compatible" loosely. Five checks separate GymKit from
            everything else:
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>Look for the words "GymKit", "Connects to Apple Watch" or "tap to connect".</strong>
            Apple says compatible equipment carries a "Connects to Apple Watch" or "Connect to Apple
            Watch" label. A listing that only says Bluetooth, FTMS or "syncs with Apple Health" is
            describing something else.</li>
            <li><strong>Check the exact model, not the range.</strong> Horizon's own table shows the 7.0
            AT with GymKit and the 7.4 AT and 7.8 AT without it. Brand-level claims are not enough.</li>
            <li><strong>Check the console version.</strong> On machines sold with a choice of consoles,
            such as the Life Fitness Run CX, Apple Watch pairing belongs to a specific console — in that
            case Track Connect 2.0.</li>
            <li><strong>Find it in the manual.</strong> A manual that tells you to turn on Detect Gym
            Equipment and hold the watch to a marked spot on the console is the clearest evidence there
            is. Most manufacturers publish manuals online, and retailers often link them.</li>
            <li><strong>Check the production year.</strong> GymKit has arrived on some models partway
            through their life. Older stock, refurbished units and second-hand machines may predate it,
            so ask the seller to confirm for the specific unit.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            Samsung Galaxy Watch users will notice that every GymKit machine here also lists Galaxy
            Watch tap-to-connect, which is handy in mixed households. That is a separate system, and
            this page did not check how well it works.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="setting-up" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Setting Up and Using GymKit at Home</h2>
<p>
            Once the treadmill is assembled, set-up takes a minute, and a few habits avoid most of the
            irritations owners report.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Turn on Detect Gym Equipment</strong> in the watch's Settings, under Workout.
            Apple's guide notes you can still pair with it off by opening the Workout app first and
            holding the watch to the reader.</li>
            <li><strong>Pair before you press Start on the treadmill.</strong> The manuals have you tap
            first and start second; the watch then waits for the treadmill to begin.</li>
            <li><strong>Hold the watch face towards the reader</strong> and keep it there for the few
            seconds the manual asks for. A quick swipe past the icon is the most common reason pairing
            fails.</li>
            <li><strong>End the workout on the treadmill</strong>, not the watch, so both sides finish
            together and the treadmill's final distance is recorded.</li>
            <li><strong>Check that the session saved</strong> in the Fitness app before you walk away,
            at least for the first few weeks.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            If you also want a third-party app on a tablet — Zwift, Kinomap or the Peloton app over
            FTMS — check how the treadmill handles two connections at once. One independent reviewer of
            the 7.0 AT reported that a chest strap and other heart rate monitors could not be used with
            third-party apps at the same time, so expect some trial and error if you want everything
            running together. For heart rate problems specifically, our guide to a
            <a href="/treadmill-heart-rate-not-working/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill heart rate that is not working</a>
            covers grips, straps and interference.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="choosing-between" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Choosing Between the Four GymKit Machines</h2>
<p>
            Because the watch pairing works the same way on all four, the choice comes down to the
            treadmill underneath, your space and the price on the day.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Bowflex T6 or Horizon 7.0 AT.</strong> These two are the default. Both have a 20
            by 60-inch deck, a 12 mph top speed, 15% incline, a 325 lb rating and a lifetime frame and
            motor warranty with three years on parts, and both come from the same parent company. Buy
            on price, and if the prices match, on console preference: Horizon's QuickDial controls and
            Bowflex's dial controls do the same job in slightly different ways. Our
            <a href="/best-horizon-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Horizon treadmill guide</a>
            sets the 7.0 AT against the rest of Horizon's range.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Bowflex T9.</strong> Choose it for the body using it, not for the watch. Two extra
            inches of deck width give more margin when your attention is on a screen or your stride
            wanders at the end of a hard session, and the 350 lb rating leaves more headroom for heavier
            users. It costs several hundred dollars more and is bigger when folded, so measure first.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Life Fitness Run CX.</strong> This is a different class of purchase: a heavy,
            non-folding machine from a gym-equipment maker, with a continuous-duty motor rating and a
            10-year motor warranty. It earns its price over many years of frequent use. If you would not
            consider a treadmill around $5,000 without GymKit, do not consider this one because of it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Space and power.</strong> All four are full-size running treadmills. Check the
            assembled footprint and the clearance behind the deck in each manual, and plan for a
            suitable outlet: Bowflex's manuals, for instance, ask for a dedicated circuit. Our guide to
            <a href="/treadmill-dimensions-space-requirements/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill dimensions and space requirements</a>
            covers the measuring.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Who Should Buy Something Else</h2>
<p>
            A GymKit treadmill is a good choice for an Apple Watch user who is buying a full-size
            treadmill anyway. It is the wrong purchase in several common situations.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you already own a treadmill that works,</strong> keep it. Record Indoor Runs,
            correct the distance when it matters, and add a foot pod if you want better pace. Replacing
            a sound treadmill for tap-to-pair is an expensive way to save a few seconds per session.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you want a big touchscreen and classes,</strong> none of the four GymKit machines
            has one; they are built around simple consoles and your own tablet. Machines built around
            iFIT, Peloton and similar platforms are covered in our
            <a href="/best-smart-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best smart treadmills</a>
            roundup. Those platforms generally do their own thing with watches, so check each one's
            current Apple Watch features before assuming.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you mostly walk at a desk,</strong> we did not find a walking pad that lists GymKit,
            and you do not need it: an Indoor Walk records steps and heart rate well enough for desk walking.
            Our <a href="/best-fitness-trackers-for-walking-pad/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">fitness trackers for walking pads</a>
            guide covers that use.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If the watch is the thing you are really shopping for,</strong> start with the
            <a href="/best-running-watches-for-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best running watches for treadmill training</a>,
            which looks at indoor distance, foot pod support and heart rate from the wrist's side of the
            problem.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which treadmills work with Apple GymKit?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Few home treadmills do. Among current models, the Bowflex T6 and T9, the Horizon 7.0 AT and the Life Fitness Run CX with the Track Connect 2.0 console are confirmed by their makers. Apple does not publish a list of compatible machines, so look for "Connects to Apple Watch" or GymKit on the exact model and console, and check the manual.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use my Apple Watch on any treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Start an Indoor Run or Indoor Walk in the Workout app on any treadmill. Heart rate is measured as usual, and distance is estimated from the watch's motion sensors because there is no GPS indoors. The estimate can be off, especially if you hold the handrails, so correct the distance at the end when it matters.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between GymKit and Bluetooth FTMS?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                GymKit pairs the watch directly with the treadmill so both share one workout: treadmill distance and speed on the watch, watch heart rate on the console. FTMS is an open Bluetooth standard that sends treadmill data to apps such as Zwift or Kinomap on a phone or tablet. A treadmill can have FTMS without GymKit, and a Bluetooth logo alone tells you neither.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is GymKit more accurate than an Indoor Run?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For distance and pace, usually, because it takes the treadmill's figures instead of estimating from your wrist. But it copies the treadmill's errors too: if the belt speed reads high, your watch records the same mistake every time. Check the belt speed with a mark and a stopwatch in the first week, and heart rate is identical either way.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does the Horizon 7.4 AT or 7.8 AT work with Apple Watch?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not by tap-to-pair. Horizon's own comparison table lists the 7.0 AT as its only current model with Apple GymKit and marks the 7.4 AT and 7.8 AT as not connecting to Apple Watch or Samsung Galaxy Watch. You can still record an Indoor Run on either, and both broadcast over Bluetooth to apps.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need Apple Fitness+ to use GymKit on a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. GymKit pairing and recording a workout need no subscription. Bowflex's manuals include separate steps for running an Apple Fitness+ treadmill workout with the watch paired, and Fitness+ is a paid Apple service, so check the current price if you want it. None of the treadmills on this page needs a subscription to work.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Buying a Treadmill for Apple Watch</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Two of these mean paying for a feature you do not get, and two mean trusting numbers that are not as good as they look.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Reading “Bluetooth” or “Apple Health” as GymKit</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Many treadmills have Bluetooth for their own app or for FTMS, and some sync to Apple Health through an app. None of that is GymKit. Look for “Connects to Apple Watch”, GymKit or tap-to-connect for the exact model, and confirm it in the owner’s manual, before paying extra for watch pairing.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming a whole range has it</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Horizon’s own table shows GymKit on the 7.0 AT and not on the 7.4 AT or 7.8 AT, and Life Fitness’s pairing belongs to the Track Connect 2.0 console only. Check the model, the console and, for older or refurbished stock, the production year rather than trusting a brand-level claim.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting paired distance without checking the belt</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">GymKit hands your watch the treadmill’s distance and pace. If the console reads fast, your history reads fast too, permanently. Mark the belt, time ten revolutions at a set speed and compare, in the first week while you can still return the machine, and recheck every few months.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Replacing a good treadmill for tap-to-pair</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">An Indoor Run works on any treadmill, and correcting the distance takes seconds. If the treadmill you own runs well, keep it and, if pace matters, add a foot pod paired to the watch. A new GymKit treadmill makes sense when you are replacing the treadmill anyway.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

